earch to minimize h(s). If the refined minimum ≤ 0, the tank can shoot.\n  - If any tank can shoot, increment count; if count exceeds k, can(v) returns false early to save time.\n- The per-tank operations mainly involve computing φi(s) via atan2 for a few hundred samples, which is acceptable in C++ with optimization. All computations use long double for better precision, and angle differences are computed with a robust normalization to the [-π, π] range.\n- Upper bound discovery: start with hi = 1 and double until can(hi) becomes true (or a very large cap). Then binary search between 0 and hi to 1e-6 precision, and print the result with 1e-4+ precision.\n\nNotes and edge considerations:\n- The problem constraints guarantee the segment AB is at least 0.1 units away from every tank, so φi(s) changes smoothly and numeric methods are stable.\n- The 0.1 second aiming guarantee implies at t=0 the required δi(0) is strictly positive, ensuring no trivial immediate shots for large v, which helps the monotonic behavior of can(v).\n- We handle the wi==0 degenerately via exact geometric intersection; wi>0 uses numeric sampling with a small local refinement to reduce risk of missing a valid t.\n- Time/memory constraints are respected with early exit when shooters > k and a reasonable M (e.g., 100) and 40 iterations of refinement.\n\nThis plan yields a complete solution with a robust numeric feasibility test and a binary search for the minimal v.\n",
