  "analysis": "We implemented a solution for CF problem 999F - Cards and Joy. The key idea is that cards are grouped by their value v and only players whose favorite is v benefit from those cards. For each value v with m_v fans (players whose favorite is v) and cnt_v cards of value v, at most min(cnt_v, m_v * k) cards of that value can be allocated to its fans (each fan can get at most k such cards). For a fixed total t of cards allocated to fans of value v, the maximum joy contributed by those m_v fans can be computed by distributing t among the m_v fans, each receiving x in [0, k], contributing h_x joy per fan. This is a bounded knapsack/DP per value: dp[i][t] = max joy after distributing to i fans with total t cards of value v. We implement a 1D optimization by iterating fans and updating dp up to cap = min(cnt_v, m_v*k). Since different values do not interact in terms of joy (non-fan cards can always fill the remaining slots without affecting joy), the total maximum joy is the sum over all values v of the best achievable joy for that value. The implementation handles edge cases (values up to 1e5, up to 5000 total cards, k up to 10) within time and memory limits. The approach matches the theoretical justification that per-value optimizations are independent and feasible due to filler cards occupying remaining slots without constraints.\n",
